Subject: [PATCH 14/15] mb/radxa/x4: Derive the SMBIOS serial number and UUID
 from the fTPM

Every unit reported the Kconfig serial number and an all-zero system
UUID.

Derive both in ramstage from a key of the Intel PTT fTPM. The board
sends TPM2_CreatePrimary in the platform hierarchy with the TCG EK
template L-2 (ECC NIST P-256), takes the public point x || y and
flushes the key. The key depends only on the platform primary seed:
TPM2_Clear keeps it, TPM2_ChangePPS replaces it. Firmware gets the
platform hierarchy with an empty password after every
TPM2_Startup(CLEAR), so nothing the OS sets can move the IDs.

If the platform key fails, the board uses the same template in the
endorsement hierarchy, then the I226 MAC address from the NIC's
receive address register. Busy TPM responses are retried for up to
200 ms, so a busy TPM does not change the source.

The material is hashed with SHA-256 together with a label for its
source, which keeps the values apart from other hashes of the key,
such as the EK certificate lookup key. The serial number is a source
prefix (X4E, X4P or X4M) and the first 64 bits of the hash in hex. The
UUID is a version 5 UUID of the hash in a namespace for this board.
Without any source, the Kconfig serial number and the all-zero UUID
stay.

The commands go through tlcl_tis_sendrecv(): the TSS has no
TPM2_CreatePrimary, and its 256-byte buffer is too small for the
response. The onboard LAN lookup moves to onboard_lan() so the SMBIOS
type 41 entry and the MAC fallback share it.

